# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 001
sp("V04-C-001", "Health and medicine",
   "Herd immunity invites free riding, which is why vaccination cannot be treated as a purely private choice.",
   "Easy",
   ["Vaccination is unusual among medical choices because its benefits do not stop at the person who actually receives the injection.",
    "When enough people in a community are immune, a pathogen struggles to find new hosts, and even those who were never vaccinated are shielded indirectly.",
    "A parent who declines a vaccine for a child may reason that, with nearly everyone else immunised, the risk of infection is already tiny.",
    "The reasoning is not foolish for any single family, which is precisely what makes it dangerous.",
    "If many families reason in the same way, the level of immunity falls below the point at which the shield holds, and outbreaks return.",
    "The protection that each free rider counted on then disappears for everyone, including the infants and the sick who could never have been vaccinated in the first place.",
    "Public health policy therefore cannot treat vaccination as a wholly private decision, even when it respects the right of individuals to make it."],
   "This indirect shield creates a temptation that any economist would recognise at once: the chance to enjoy a benefit without paying its cost.",
   [2, 4, 5, 7],
   "The missing sentence names 'This indirect shield', which points back to the second sentence, where the unvaccinated are 'shielded indirectly'. It then announces a temptation to enjoy a benefit without paying for it, and the very next sentence gives that temptation in action: the parent who reasons that everyone else is already immunised. Only gap (1) sits between the shield and the parent's reasoning.",
   {4: "At gap (2) the parent's reasoning has already been described and judged dangerous, so announcing the temptation now comes after its own example, and it would cut the link between 'what makes it dangerous' and the explanation that follows.",
    5: "At gap (3) the sentence would split 'If many families reason in the same way ... outbreaks return' from 'The protection ... then disappears', two halves of a single chain of consequence.",
    7: "At gap (4) the paragraph has already drawn its policy conclusion with 'therefore', so a sentence introducing the temptation would arrive after the argument it sets up has finished."},
   "Choosing gap (2) because the sentence sounds like a general comment on the parent's reasoning, without noticing that it introduces that reasoning rather than following it.",
   "V04C-herd-immunity-free-riders",
   "A clear backward reference ('this indirect shield') and a forward set-up for the example make the position easy to locate.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 002
sp("V04-C-002", "Health and medicine",
   "Letting patients finish their opening account is part of diagnosis, not a courtesy that competes with it.",
   "Medium",
   ["Medical training has long prized the ability to reach a diagnosis efficiently, and the classic consultation is built around the doctor's questions rather than the patient's story.",
    "Efficiency of this kind has obvious virtues in a crowded clinic, where every minute spent with one patient is a minute taken from the next.",
    "Yet the habit of steering the conversation early can cost more time than it saves, because the complaint a patient mentions first is not always the one that matters most.",
    "Cut off early, such patients leave with a remedy for whatever they happened to mention first, and the problem that really brought them in waits for another visit, or for an emergency.",
    "Allowing people to finish their opening account need not take long, and the fear that it will swallow the whole appointment is usually exaggerated.",
    "The deeper lesson is that listening is not a courtesy added to diagnosis but one of its instruments, as much a part of the examination as the stethoscope."],
   "A woman who begins by asking about a persistent cough, for instance, may reach her real worry, a lump she has been afraid to name, only if she is allowed to keep talking.",
   [2, 3, 5, 6],
   "The missing sentence is an example marked by 'for instance': a patient whose first complaint, the cough, hides the one that matters, the lump. It therefore illustrates the claim in the third sentence that 'the complaint a patient mentions first is not always the one that matters most', and it prepares 'such patients' and 'whatever they happened to mention first' in the sentence after. That places it at gap (2).",
   {2: "At gap (1) the claim about hidden complaints has not yet been made, and the example would separate the praise of efficiency from the 'Yet' that answers it.",
    5: "At gap (3) the paragraph has moved on to whether listening takes too long; an example about a concealed worry no longer has a claim beside it to illustrate.",
    6: "At gap (4) the paragraph has reached its general lesson, and an illustration introduced with 'for instance' cannot come after the conclusion it would support."},
   "Picking gap (3) because the example ends with the patient being 'allowed to keep talking', which seems to echo the sentence about letting people finish, though that sentence concerns time, not hidden complaints.",
   "V04C-doctors-listening-to-patients",
   "The example could be attached to either of two sentences about listening; the solver must see that it illustrates the claim about first complaints, not the claim about time.")

