sp("V04-C-003", "Health and medicine",
   "Fever is a deliberate defence of the body, though there remain cases in which treating it is clearly right.",
   "Medium",
   ["For much of the modern era, fever has been treated as an enemy, something to be brought down as soon as a thermometer confirmed it.",
    "The instinct is understandable, since a high temperature is uncomfortable, frightening in a child and, at its extremes, genuinely dangerous.",
    "Yet a rise in body temperature is not simply a malfunction; it is a response the body produces on purpose, at real cost in energy, when it detects infection.",
    "Many microbes multiply less efficiently when the body is warmer, and parts of the immune system appear to work faster under the same conditions.",
    "On this view, routinely suppressing a moderate fever may resemble switching off an alarm because its noise is unpleasant.",
    "A very high temperature, a fever in a newborn, or one in a patient with a weak heart can do harm of its own, and in such cases treatment is plainly right.",
    "The sensible position lies between reflexive suppression and romantic tolerance: fever is one of the body's tools, and like any tool it can be misused."],
   "None of this means that a fever should be left alone in every case.",
   [1, 3, 5, 7],
   "The missing sentence is a concession: 'None of this' looks back on the whole case for fever as a defence, and 'should be left alone in every case' looks forward to the exceptions. The sixth sentence lists exactly those exceptions, and the case for fever is complete only after the alarm comparison. The sentence therefore belongs at gap (3), where it turns the argument from the case for fever to its limits.",
   {1: "At gap (1) only the old view of fever as an enemy has been stated, so there is no case for leaving fever alone for 'None of this' to qualify.",
    3: "At gap (2) the concession would interrupt the argument just as the evidence for the deliberate response is about to be given, and the paragraph would then return to defending fever with 'On this view', leaving the exceptions without any turn to introduce them.",
    7: "At gap (4) the balanced conclusion has already been drawn and the exceptions already listed, so a concession placed after them would be redundant and out of order."},
   "Choosing gap (2), on the thought that a qualification can come straight after the claim it qualifies, without noticing that the exceptions it introduces appear only later.",
   "V04C-fever-as-defence",
   "The solver must see that the concession must sit directly before the list of exceptions, after the full case for fever, rather than right after the first claim.")

