sp("V04-C-004", "Health and medicine",
   "Opt-out organ donation cannot claim to record consent; its honest justification is the lives it saves.",
   "Difficult",
   ["Switching organ donation from an opt-in to an opt-out system is often presented as a costless gain: nobody is forced to donate, yet far more people end up on the register.",
    "The argument relies on a familiar observation about defaults, namely that most people stay with whatever option has been chosen for them if they do nothing.",
    "But the very inertia that makes the policy effective also makes its moral credentials uncertain.",
    "If people remain on the donor register mainly because they never got round to leaving it, their silence is weak evidence of what they actually want.",
    "Defenders reply that the same is true of the opt-in system, where many who would gladly donate are never counted because they never filled in a form.",
    "This reply has some force, but it shows only that neither default reads wishes off behaviour reliably, not that the opt-out default reads them better."],
   "Since both defaults must then guess at what silence means, the honest case for presumed consent rests on the lives it saves rather than on any claim to have discovered what people want.",
   [2, 3, 5, 6],
   "The missing sentence draws a conclusion ('then') from the point that 'both defaults' must guess at the meaning of silence. That point is established only by the last sentence, which answers the defenders' reply by showing that 'neither default' reads wishes reliably. The conclusion must therefore come after that sentence, at gap (4).",
   {2: "At gap (1) the paragraph has only set out the case for the policy; no comparison of the two defaults has been made, so 'both defaults' has nothing to rest on, and the sentence would also stand between that case and the 'But' that answers it.",
    3: "At gap (2) the doubt about inertia has only just been raised; the case for resting the policy on saved lives cannot be concluded before the objection, the defenders' reply and the answer to that reply have been weighed.",
    5: "At gap (3) the defenders have just mentioned the opt-in system, but the sentence would come between their reply and 'This reply has some force', so 'This reply' would no longer point to the defenders' argument."},
   "Choosing gap (3) because the defenders' sentence is the first to bring in the opt-in system, so 'both defaults' seems to have an antecedent there; the conclusion actually depends on the final sentence's judgement of that reply.",
   "V04C-opt-out-organ-donation",
   "Gap (3) is strongly tempting because the comparison of defaults begins there; only by following the objection, reply and answer to the reply does the solver see that the conclusion must come last.",
   exams=("CAT", "XAT"))

