sp("V04-C-006", "History and politics",
   "Term limits restrain leaders but may also weaken accountability in a leader's final term.",
   "Medium",
   ["Term limits are usually defended as a safeguard against the slow accumulation of power by a single leader.",
    "A ruler who knows that office will end on a fixed date has less reason to pack the courts, intimidate the press or rewrite the rules in their own favour.",
    "The argument is persuasive, and in many constitutions the limit has served precisely that purpose.",
    "Critics, though, point out that a limit also removes the voters' power to keep a leader with whom they are satisfied.",
    "Seen this way, the rule protects democracy by restricting one of its choices, an awkward position for a democratic principle to occupy.",
    "A leader who cannot stand again may stop worrying about the next election altogether, so that a final term becomes a period of reduced accountability rather than renewed restraint.",
    "Whether the limit restrains power or merely moves the danger into the last years in office depends heavily on the other checks that surround it."],
   "There is, however, a less obvious cost that both sides of this debate tend to overlook.",
   [1, 3, 4, 5],
   "The missing sentence announces a 'less obvious cost' overlooked by 'both sides of this debate'. Both sides, the defence of limits and the critics' objection, are complete only after the fifth sentence, and the cost being announced is the one described next: a final term with reduced accountability. Only gap (4) lies between the two.",
   {1: "At gap (1) only the defence of term limits has been stated, so there is no debate with two sides yet.",
    3: "At gap (2) the critics have not yet spoken, and the sentence that follows, 'Critics, though, point out ...', gives a familiar objection, not the overlooked cost the missing sentence promises.",
    4: "At gap (3) the sentence would separate the critics' point from 'Seen this way', which develops it, and what follows would still be the critics' well-known objection rather than a less obvious cost."},
   "Choosing gap (2) because the critics' objection looks like the promised 'cost', when in fact it is the second side of the debate the sentence refers to.",
   "V04C-term-limits-rulers",
   "The sentence sets up what follows it; the solver must distinguish the critics' familiar objection from the overlooked cost of the final term.")

