sp("V04-C-007", "History and politics",
   "Sumptuary laws expressed both a fear of ruinous display and a wish to keep rank visible, probably as one impulse.",
   "Difficult",
   ["Sumptuary laws, which in many premodern societies dictated who might wear silk, fur or particular colours, look to modern eyes like snobbery written into statute.",
    "Their texts complain repeatedly of fortunes wasted on finery, of households impoverished by competitive display, and of money leaving the realm to pay for foreign luxuries.",
    "Seen in this light, the laws were partly an attempt to halt an arms race of appearance, in which each family felt obliged to match the display of its neighbours.",
    "That does not make them benign, for they also fixed the visible markers of rank and punished those who tried to blur them.",
    "The two motives were probably entangled, since a society that feared ruinous competition also feared the confusion of status that such competition produced.",
    "What modern readers see as snobbery and what lawmakers described as prudence may in the end have been a single impulse viewed from two sides."],
   "Read closely, however, many of them reveal an anxiety less about the poor dressing above their station than about the prosperous spending themselves into ruin.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "The missing sentence turns, with 'however', against the first impression of 'snobbery written into statute', and it states the reading that the next sentence supports with evidence: complaints about fortunes wasted and households impoverished. It must therefore come between the first impression and that evidence, at gap (1).",
   {2: "At gap (2) the evidence of waste has already been given, so 'however' would contrast with a sentence it agrees with, and the claim would follow its own support instead of introducing it.",
    3: "At gap (3) the sentence would come between 'Seen in this light' and 'That does not make them benign', which answers the arms race reading directly, and it would merely repeat that reading.",
    5: "At gap (4) the paragraph has already said the two motives were entangled, so a one-sided claim that the laws were about spending 'less' than about rank would contradict the synthesis it follows."},
   "Choosing gap (2), because the sentence seems to sum up the complaints about waste, without noticing that 'however' needs the contrast with the first impression of snobbery.",
   "V04C-sumptuary-laws",
   "Gap (2) is close to the true gap and the sentence seems to summarise the evidence there; only the contrast marker and the claim-before-evidence order separate them.",
   exams=("CAT", "XAT"))

