sp("V04-C-008", "History and politics",
   "The secret ballot protected elections by removing the means by which the powerful could punish or buy votes.",
   "Medium",
   ["For much of the history of elections, voting was a public act, in which electors declared their choice aloud or marked a ballot under the eyes of others.",
    "Some defenders of open voting held that this was a virtue, since a citizen who voted in public would have to stand behind the choice and answer for it.",
    "The argument assumed that the main pressure on a voter came from conscience and the opinion of fellow citizens.",
    "In practice, the pressure that counted most often came from those who had power over the voter's livelihood.",
    "Secrecy did not make voters braver; it made courage unnecessary, by removing the means through which their choices could be punished.",
    "It also made bribery less attractive, since a buyer could no longer check whether the vote he had paid for was actually delivered.",
    "The private booth, in other words, served the public purpose of elections by making the individual act invisible."],
   "A tenant who voted against his landlord's candidate, or a labourer who defied his employer, could lose his home or his wage soon afterwards.",
   [1, 2, 4, 6],
   "The missing sentence is a concrete case of the pressure described in the fourth sentence, 'those who had power over the voter's livelihood': the landlord can take the home and the employer the wage. It also prepares the next sentence, where secrecy removes 'the means through which their choices could be punished'. It belongs at gap (3).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the paragraph has only described public voting; the case of the tenant would interrupt 'this was a virtue', which must follow the description of open voting directly.",
    2: "At gap (2) the sentence would separate the defenders' view from 'The argument assumed', which must follow that view, and no claim about pressure from the powerful has yet been made.",
    6: "At gap (4) the paragraph has moved on to bribery, and a case of intimidation placed there would have no claim beside it to illustrate."},
   "Choosing gap (2), on the idea that the tenant's case answers the defenders of open voting, before the paragraph has identified pressure from employers and landlords.",
   "V04C-secret-ballot",
   "An example placed after the claim it illustrates; the solver must match 'power over the voter's livelihood' with home and wage.")

