sp("V04-C-011", "Cities and geography",
   "The demolition of city walls opened cities to light and space but also to closer supervision by the state.",
   "Difficult",
   ["When the old walls of many European cities came down in the nineteenth century, the change was widely celebrated as a liberation.",
    "The walls had lost most of their military value, and they were blamed for crowding, foul air and the high price of land within them.",
    "The ground they freed, however, was not simply handed back to the townspeople who had lived in their shadow.",
    "The cleared belt proved useful to governments in ways that had little to do with fresh air: wide straight roads were easy to patrol and hard to barricade, and the buildings along them displayed official power.",
    "Opening a city, in other words, could also mean opening it to closer supervision.",
    "None of this makes the removal of the walls a mistake, since the gains in space and health were real.",
    "It does suggest that urban improvements should be asked not only what they remove but whose purposes the cleared space is made to serve."],
   "Much of it became broad avenues, parks and imposing public buildings, laid out by authorities who now had, for the first time, a large empty belt to design at will.",
   [1, 3, 4, 5],
   "'Much of it' needs a stretch of ground as its antecedent, and the third sentence supplies it: 'The ground they freed ... was not simply handed back'. That sentence says what did not happen to the ground; the missing sentence says what did, and it introduces the 'large empty belt' and the avenues and buildings that the next sentence takes up as 'The cleared belt', 'wide straight roads' and 'the buildings along them'. Only gap (2) gives both links.",
   {1: "At gap (1) no freed ground has been mentioned yet, so 'Much of it' could refer only to the celebration or the change, and the paragraph has not yet said who received the land.",
    4: "At gap (3) the use of the roads for patrol has already been described, so the avenues would be introduced after their purpose, and the sentence would split that description from 'in other words', which restates it.",
    5: "At gap (4) the paragraph has reached its first conclusion about supervision; 'Much of it' would have to refer to supervision, which makes no sense, and the concession that follows needs that conclusion right before it."},
   "Choosing gap (3) because 'Much of it' seems to fit 'The cleared belt' just before it, overlooking that the avenues and buildings are introduced too late there and that 'in other words' must follow the patrol sentence directly.",
   "V04C-city-walls-demolition",
   "Two nearby antecedents ('The ground they freed' and 'The cleared belt') compete for 'Much of it'; the forward link to the roads and buildings decides it.",
   exams=("CAT", "XAT"))

