sp("V04-C-012", "Cities and geography",
   "The openness of port cities is a habit born of trade, and it can fade when the trade does.",
   "Easy",
   ["Port cities have long had a reputation for being more open to outsiders than the inland towns around them.",
    "The reason is less a matter of temperament than of trade: a harbour brings strangers every week, and a merchant who refuses to deal with foreigners soon has nobody to deal with.",
    "Over generations such cities develop habits that make strangeness ordinary, from markets where several languages are spoken to courts that learn to settle disputes between parties from distant places.",
    "Their food, their slang and even their buildings often carry traces of the many shores their ships have touched.",
    "This openness, however, is a habit born of usefulness, and it can fade when the usefulness does.",
    "Cosmopolitanism of this kind is real, but it is maintained by circumstance rather than guaranteed by character."],
   "A port whose harbour silts up or whose trade moves to a rival can turn inward within a lifetime, its old tolerance surviving mainly in its street names and recipes.",
   [1, 2, 4, 5],
   "The missing sentence is an example of the claim in the fifth sentence that openness 'can fade when the usefulness does': a harbour that silts up or loses its trade loses its tolerance. An example follows the claim it illustrates, and the final sentence then generalises from it. The sentence belongs at gap (4).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the paragraph has only stated the reputation of ports for openness, so an example of a port turning inward has no claim to illustrate.",
    2: "At gap (2) the paragraph is explaining how trade creates openness; a case of decline would interrupt that explanation before any loss has been mentioned.",
    4: "At gap (3) the paragraph is still describing what openness leaves behind; the example of decline would come before 'This openness, however', the claim that openness can fade."},
   "Choosing gap (3) because 'street names and recipes' echoes the traces of food and buildings in the fourth sentence, when the example is about decline, which is introduced only in the fifth.",
   "V04C-port-cities-cosmopolitanism",
   "The example clearly illustrates one claim, and the only tempting wrong gap relies on a surface echo.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

