sp("V04-C-013", "Philosophy and ethics",
   "The puzzle that future people owe their existence to our choices does not dissolve our duties to them, though it changes whom they are owed to.",
   "Difficult",
   ["It is often said that we owe things to future generations: a habitable climate, fertile soils and institutions that still work.",
    "A well-known puzzle complicates this thought, because the particular people who will exist in a century depend on countless choices made now, including choices about how we live.",
    "Had we acted differently, different couples would have met at different times, and a different set of people would have been born.",
    "It seems to follow that a careless policy cannot harm those future people, since without it they would not have existed at all.",
    "One response is that our duty is owed not to particular individuals but to whoever comes after us, much as a builder owes safe stairs to whoever will one day climb them.",
    "On that view, harm need not be done to a specific person to be a wrong; it is enough that the world we hand on is worse than one we could easily have left.",
    "The puzzle, then, does not dissolve our obligations, though it does change our sense of whom they are owed to."],
   "Yet few people are willing to accept that conclusion, and their reluctance deserves to be taken seriously rather than dismissed as confusion.",
   [1, 3, 4, 6],
   "'that conclusion' must be something the paragraph has just inferred, and the only inference is the fourth sentence: 'It seems to follow that a careless policy cannot harm those future people'. The missing sentence resists it with 'Yet' and takes the resistance seriously, which is what the fifth sentence then does by offering 'One response'. It belongs at gap (3).",
   {1: "At gap (1) only the common claim about duties has been stated; rejecting it as 'that conclusion' would undercut the paragraph's own opening, and no puzzle has yet been raised.",
    3: "At gap (2) the paragraph has just given a premise, that different people would have been born, which is a plain fact rather than a conclusion; the missing sentence would also split that premise from 'It seems to follow', which draws the inference from it.",
    6: "At gap (4) 'that conclusion' would refer to the builder view of duties, so the paragraph would reject the very response it then endorses in its final sentence with 'then'."},
   "Choosing gap (2) because the claim that different people would have been born sounds surprising enough to be resisted, though it is a premise and the conclusion is drawn only in the next sentence.",
   "V04C-duties-future-generations",
   "The argument is abstract and the solver must separate a premise from the conclusion drawn from it; two gaps each follow a sentence that could loosely be called a conclusion.",
   exams=("CAT", "XAT"))

