sp("V04-C-015", "Philosophy and ethics",
   "Even effort owes something to luck, so claims of desert should be made with humility rather than abandoned.",
   "Medium",
   ["We commonly say that people who work hard deserve their success, and the claim seems hardest to dispute when the success is the reward of visible effort.",
    "A surgeon who trained for many years, or a musician who has practised every morning since childhood, appears to have earned whatever recognition follows.",
    "If the willingness to persevere is partly a matter of luck, then success built on it is not purely earned either.",
    "This does not show that nobody deserves anything, for the argument, pushed far enough, would leave no room for praise or blame of any kind.",
    "What it shows is narrower: claims of desert should be made with some humility, since nobody chose all the conditions of their own diligence.",
    "Gratitude, on this view, is as fitting a response to one's success as pride."],
   "Yet the capacity for such effort is itself unevenly distributed, shaped by temperament, health and the encouragement a child happened to receive.",
   [1, 2, 4, 5],
   "The missing sentence turns ('Yet') against the surgeon and the musician, whose 'effort' it calls into question, and it supplies the premise of the next sentence: if the capacity for effort depends on temperament, health and encouragement, then 'the willingness to persevere is partly a matter of luck'. It belongs at gap (2).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the sentence would interrupt the claim about deserved success before the surgeon and the musician illustrate it, so the examples of earned recognition would follow the objection instead of the claim they support.",
    4: "At gap (3) the paragraph has already concluded that effort-based success is not purely earned and has begun to limit that conclusion, so the premise would arrive after both, and it would split 'This does not show' from 'What it shows is narrower', which completes it.",
    5: "At gap (4) the narrower lesson has already been drawn, so reopening the premise about unequal capacity would reverse the order of argument and conclusion."},
   "Choosing gap (1) because 'such effort' can point back to the 'visible effort' of the first sentence, overlooking that the examples must follow the claim they illustrate.",
   "V04C-moral-desert-talent",
   "'Such effort' has a possible antecedent in two places; the contrast marker and the conditional that follows decide the gap.")

