sp("V04-C-017", "Psychology",
   "Hindsight makes outcomes look foreseeable, so fair judgement of a decision requires seeing it as it looked beforehand.",
   "Easy",
   ["After the result of an event is known, people find it oddly difficult to recall how open the matter seemed while it was still undecided.",
    "A business failure, a lost election or a medical complication can seem, in retrospect, to have been plainly foreseeable.",
    "We recall the warning signs that pointed towards the outcome and forget the many others that pointed elsewhere.",
    "This tendency is harmless in conversation, but it becomes unfair when we use it to judge people who had to decide without knowing the ending.",
    "A doctor whose reasonable treatment went wrong, or a manager whose sensible gamble failed, may be blamed for missing what was never clear at the time.",
    "Those who investigate accidents face the same temptation, since a chain of causes always looks tidier once it has been traced from the end."],
   "Whether the decision was taken in a clinic, an office or the moments before an accident, then, fairness requires us to see it as it looked before the outcome was known, not as it looks afterwards.",
   [1, 3, 4, 6],
   "The missing sentence is the conclusion of the paragraph, signalled by 'then': having shown that hindsight distorts our view and leads us to blame people unfairly, the paragraph states how a decision ought to be judged. Its 'clinic, an office or the moments before an accident' gathers up the doctor, the manager and the accident investigators, the last of whom appear only in the final sentence, so it belongs at the end, gap (4).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the paragraph has only stated that knowing the result distorts memory; nothing yet has been said about judging decisions, so 'then' would draw a conclusion with no argument behind it.",
    3: "At gap (2) the paragraph is still explaining how the distortion works; concluding here would come before the point that it makes judgement of others unfair, and it would separate 'This tendency' from the sentence it refers to.",
    4: "At gap (3) the claim of unfairness has just been made, but its examples, the doctor, the manager and the investigators, have not yet been given, so the clinic, the office and the accident that the sentence sums up would not yet have been mentioned, and the conclusion would break off the claim from its illustrations."},
   "Choosing gap (3) because the sentence follows naturally from the claim of unfairness, without noticing that the clinic, the office and the accident it gathers up are the examples still to come.",
   "V04C-hindsight-bias",
   "A clear concluding sentence with 'then' that sums up all three examples; the only competing gap cuts a claim off from its examples.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

