sp("V04-C-018", "Psychology",
   "Memory rebuilds events and can absorb later suggestions, so the way witnesses are questioned shapes their testimony.",
   "Medium",
   ["We tend to think of memory as a recording, so that a confident eyewitness seems to be simply replaying what was seen.",
    "Much research on memory points to a different picture, in which each act of remembering partly rebuilds the event from stored fragments.",
    "A leading question, a photograph seen in a newspaper or a conversation with another witness can slip details into the reconstruction, which the witness then remembers as part of the original scene.",
    "Confidence offers little protection, since a memory that has been rehearsed many times may grow more vivid and more certain as it grows less accurate.",
    "None of this means that witnesses are usually wrong, and many recall the central facts of an event perfectly well.",
    "It does mean that the way a witness is questioned can shape the testimony later given, and that confidence in court should be weighed with some caution."],
   "This rebuilding matters most in precisely the situations where we rely on witnesses, because it is open to influence from whatever happens after the event.",
   [1, 2, 4, 5],
   "'This rebuilding' points back to the claim that remembering 'partly rebuilds the event', and the sentence then sets up what follows: the reconstruction is 'open to influence from whatever happens after the event'. The next sentence lists exactly such influences, a leading question, a photograph, another witness. The sentence belongs at gap (2).",
   {1: "At gap (1) memory has been described only as a recording, so 'This rebuilding' has nothing to refer to.",
    4: "At gap (3) the influences after the event have already been listed and the point about confidence made, so a sentence announcing that the reconstruction is open to later influence would arrive after its own illustration.",
    5: "At gap (4) the sentence would split 'None of this means' from 'It does mean', a pair that must stay together, and it would return to the mechanism after the paragraph has turned to its conclusion."},
   "Choosing gap (3) because the sentence seems to explain why confidence offers little protection, though what it sets up is the list of influences in the next sentence.",
   "V04C-eyewitness-memory",
   "A backward reference and a forward set-up together fix the gap; the tempting wrong gap follows the sentence on confidence, which also concerns later influence.")

