sp("V04-C-020", "Psychology",
   "Familiarity breeds a mild preference grounded in a sense of safety, which is different from the delight that keeps things loved.",
   "Difficult",
   ["People tend to like things more the more often they encounter them, even when they cannot recall having encountered them at all.",
    "A tune heard repeatedly in the background, a face glimpsed on a daily commute, an unfamiliar word printed again and again: each tends to grow more agreeable simply by becoming familiar.",
    "The effect is often invoked to explain why advertisers repeat the same slogans and why voters warm to candidates whose names they have seen on many posters.",
    "On one account, familiarity works as a signal of safety, since something met many times without harm is probably not dangerous.",
    "The account also fits a limit the effect seems to have: once a thing has become thoroughly familiar, further repetition adds little and may begin to bore.",
    "That is why a slogan can become agreeable without ever becoming loved, and why the things people cherish most tend to offer some surprise each time they return."],
   "Safety, however, is not the same as delight, and a preference that repetition first builds and then wears out is a mild liking, not real enthusiasm.",
   [2, 3, 4, 5],
   "The missing sentence qualifies ('however') the safety account by separating safety from delight, and it describes the preference as one 'that repetition first builds and then wears out', which presupposes the limit described in the fifth sentence, where further repetition 'adds little and may begin to bore'. The last sentence then depends on the distinction it draws: 'That is why a slogan can become agreeable without ever becoming loved'. Only gap (4) comes after the limit and before that conclusion.",
   {2: "At gap (1) safety has not yet been mentioned, so 'Safety, however' has nothing to qualify, and the sentence would interrupt the examples before they are applied to advertising.",
    3: "At gap (2) the paragraph has discussed advertising and posters, but the safety account appears only in the next sentence, so the qualification would come before the idea it qualifies.",
    4: "At gap (3) the safety account has just been given, but repetition has not yet been shown to wear the preference out; that happens only in the next sentence, so the missing sentence would describe the limit before the paragraph introduces it, and 'The account also fits' would no longer follow the account directly."},
   "Choosing gap (3), right after the safety account, because a 'however' qualification seems to belong next to the claim it qualifies, without noticing that the preference 'that repetition first builds and then wears out' depends on the limit described in the next sentence.",
   "V04C-mere-exposure-familiarity",
   "Gap (3) is strongly tempting because the qualification seems to belong beside the safety account; the solver must notice that the sentence already presupposes the limit described one sentence later.",
   exams=("CAT", "XAT"))

