sp("V04-C-022", "Society and culture",
   "School uniforms move signals of wealth rather than abolish them, so the case for uniforms should rest on other grounds.",
   "Medium",
   ["A common case for uniforms in schools is that they hide visible differences of wealth among pupils, sparing poorer children the embarrassment of being seen in cheaper clothes.",
    "The argument has real force in schools where fashion has become a form of competition, and where the cost of keeping up falls hardest on families who can least afford it.",
    "Shoes, bags, phones, haircuts and even the newness of the uniform itself continue to tell pupils who has money and who does not.",
    "A uniform may also bring costs of its own, since the prescribed items are sometimes sold only through particular suppliers at prices well above those of ordinary clothing.",
    "None of this shows that uniforms are pointless, for many pupils and parents value the sense of belonging they create.",
    "It does show that the case for them should rest on belonging and convenience rather than on the promise of hiding inequality, a promise they keep only in part."],
   "Uniforms, however, do not abolish the signals of wealth so much as move them to places that a dress code does not reach.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "The missing sentence turns ('however') against the case that uniforms hide visible differences of wealth, after the paragraph has granted that argument 'real force'. It makes the general claim that signals of wealth move elsewhere, and the next sentence lists where they move: shoes, bags, phones, haircuts. It belongs at gap (2).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the sentence would come between the case for uniforms and 'The argument has real force', which must follow it, and the concession would then lose its object.",
    3: "At gap (3) the list of shoes, bags and phones has already been given, so the claim would follow its own evidence, and 'however' would contrast with a sentence that agrees with it.",
    5: "At gap (4) the sentence would split 'None of this shows' from 'It does show', which must stay together."},
   "Choosing gap (3) because the sentence seems to sum up the list of signals, without noticing that 'however' needs a view to contrast with, and the list already supports the point.",
   "V04C-school-uniforms",
   "The contrast marker must answer the case for uniforms, and the wrong gap after the list tempts solvers who read the sentence as a summary.")

