sp("V04-C-023", "Society and culture",
   "The link between family meals and children's welfare may reflect household conditions, so advice to eat together should be modest.",
   "Medium",
   ["It is often observed that children who regularly eat meals with their families tend to fare better, at school and elsewhere, than children who rarely do.",
    "From this a simple prescription is drawn: families should eat together more often, and the benefits will follow.",
    "The prescription assumes that the meal itself produces the benefit, when it may merely mark households that already enjoy other advantages.",
    "Parents with regular working hours, enough money and a calm home find shared meals easier to arrange, and those same conditions help children in many other ways.",
    "Telling families with irregular hours to eat together therefore adds a burden without touching the conditions that actually matter.",
    "None of this means that shared meals are worthless, since conversation across a table may well do some good of its own.",
    "But the good is likely to be smaller than the bare association suggests, and the advice deserves to be offered with matching modesty."],
   "A household in which both parents work night shifts lacks the first of those conditions, yet it may be close and loving and still almost never sit down to dinner together.",
   [1, 2, 4, 6],
   "The missing sentence is an example that cuts against the prescription: a loving family that cannot eat together because of its working hours. Its 'the first of those conditions' points back to the 'regular working hours' listed in the fourth sentence, so it illustrates the point just made, that regular hours and other conditions make shared meals easier, by showing a family without them, and it prepares the next sentence about 'families with irregular hours'. It belongs at gap (3).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the sentence would separate the observation from 'From this a simple prescription is drawn', which must follow it, and no conditions have been listed for 'the first of those conditions' to refer to.",
    2: "At gap (2) no conditions have yet been listed, so 'the first of those conditions' would have nothing to refer to; regular working hours are named only in the fourth sentence.",
    6: "At gap (4) the sentence would split the concession that shared meals are not worthless from the 'But' that limits it, and the night shift case would no longer be beside the sentence about working hours."},
   "Choosing gap (2) because the night shift family seems an obvious objection to the prescription, overlooking that 'the first of those conditions' needs the list of household conditions given only in the fourth sentence.",
   "V04C-shared-family-meals",
   "An example after the claim it illustrates, with a tempting gap where the example looks like a direct objection to the prescription.")

