sp("V04-C-024", "Society and culture",
   "Fashions in children's names rise and fall without any seller, driven by each parent's private wish for a name both fresh and familiar.",
   "Difficult",
   ["Names for children rise and fall in popularity in waves that look remarkably like fashions in clothing, yet no industry advertises them and no designer sets the trend.",
    "Parents usually want a name that sounds fresh without sounding strange, and they judge freshness largely by how rarely they have heard it among the children they know.",
    "A name that is rising satisfies both demands at once, which is why more and more parents converge on it.",
    "That very convergence destroys its appeal, since a name heard in every playground no longer sounds fresh to the next set of parents.",
    "The name then declines, and after two or three generations it begins to sound not dated but quaint, ready to be rediscovered.",
    "In this way a trend can rise and fall without anyone directing it, driven only by many private choices that each respond to the choices of others."],
   "The waves seem to arise instead from a tension that each parent feels privately, with no one setting out to start or end them.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "The missing sentence answers the puzzle in the first sentence, that no industry or designer drives names: 'instead' contrasts with those absent sources, and 'a tension that each parent feels privately' is announced before the next sentence spells it out as the wish for a name 'fresh without sounding strange'. It must sit between the puzzle and that explanation, at gap (1).",
   {2: "At gap (2) the tension between freshness and strangeness has already been described, so announcing it would come after its own content, and 'instead' would be separated from the industry and designer it contrasts with.",
    3: "At gap (3) the sentence would come between the convergence on a rising name and 'That very convergence', which must follow it directly.",
    5: "At gap (4) the full cycle has been traced, and the final sentence already summarises it with 'In this way'; announcing the private tension here would repeat what the paragraph explained four sentences earlier, as though for the first time."},
   "Choosing gap (4) because the sentence sounds like a summary of the cycle, overlooking that it introduces the tension, which the second sentence then explains, and that 'instead' answers the opening puzzle.",
   "V04C-baby-name-fashions",
   "Both gap (2) and gap (4) offer a plausible role, as a gloss on the tension or as a summary; the solver must see that the sentence announces the tension and answers the opening with 'instead'.",
   exams=("CAT", "XAT"))

