sp("V04-C-025", "Society and culture",
   "Because work supplies structure and identity, preparing for retirement involves more than saving money.",
   "Easy",
   ["For many people, work supplies far more than an income: it gives the day a shape, provides a circle of colleagues and answers the question of what one does.",
    "Retirement removes all of these at once, which helps to explain why a change that people look forward to for years can leave them unsettled when it finally arrives.",
    "Those who adjust most easily are often not simply the ones with the largest savings but the ones who built a life outside their job before they left it.",
    "Friendships formed away from the office, a skill pursued for its own sake or a role in a neighbourhood group can all carry over when the job ends.",
    "Others find that the freedom they had imagined feels at first more like an absence, and they must slowly build for themselves the structure that work once provided."],
   "Preparing for retirement, then, is not only a matter of money but of sparing oneself that slow rebuilding by starting a life beyond the job while the job still lasts.",
   [1, 2, 4, 5],
   "The missing sentence draws the conclusion ('then') of the whole paragraph: since work provides structure and identity, and since those who adjust well had a life outside work while others must rebuild one, preparation must go beyond money. Its 'that slow rebuilding' points back to the last sentence, where others 'must slowly build for themselves the structure that work once provided', so it belongs at the end, gap (4).",
   {1: "At gap (1) only the functions of work have been listed; nothing has yet been said about adjusting to retirement, so 'then' would conclude too early, and 'that slow rebuilding' would have nothing to refer to.",
    2: "At gap (2) 'that slow rebuilding' has no antecedent, since the slow building of a new structure is described only in the last sentence, and the comparison of those who adjust easily with those who do not, on which the conclusion rests, has not yet been made.",
    4: "At gap (3) the sentence would separate the people who adjust easily from 'Others find', which contrasts with them and must follow their description."},
   "Choosing gap (3) because the conclusion seems to follow from the examples of a life outside work, overlooking that 'Others' must come straight after them.",
   "V04C-retirement-and-identity",
   "A plain concluding sentence whose 'that slow rebuilding' points to the last sentence; the only tempting gap breaks an obvious 'Those ... Others' contrast.",
   cognitive="Understanding")
